Strabismus
Paediatric & Neonatal
Visible ocular misalignment, Asymmetrical corneal light reflex, Refixation on cover test

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: PDN-047
Reason for Visit: Acute parental panic regarding sudden, new-onset eye crossing and clumsy behavior following a mild viral illness, requiring urgent evaluation to rule out an intracranial mass or acute cranial nerve palsy.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and parents
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage documented a previously well 3-year-old who suddenly developed a severely crossed eye tonight and is now walking into furniture. Immediate assessment was required to definitively exclude acute raised intracranial pressure, an expanding posterior fossa tumor, or a severe post-viral neurological complication prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "Her left eye just turned completely inward after dinner, and now she keeps missing her cup when she reaches for it."
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with objective, visible ocular misalignment consistently manifesting as esotropia of the left eye. The onset was acutely noticed by parents this evening. There is a clear loss of functional stereopsis, definitively resulting in severely compromised binocular depth perception, which explains the child's sudden clumsiness and reaching errors. The child has been squinting and tilting her head, explicitly attempting to mechanically optimize the visual axis. The parents report an intermittent, transient drifting of the eye strictly associated with periods of severe physical fatigue over the last month, but tonight the deviation became fixed. The child had a mild upper respiratory infection three days ago.
Functional Status: Visually disoriented, repeatedly closing one eye to focus.
Social/Safety Concerns: Parental terror regarding a presumed brain tumor.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 95/60 | HR: 110 | Temp: 36.7C | RR: 20 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 15 Kg
General: Alert, well-perfused, frequently rubbing the left eye, physically unsteady when walking.
Physical Exam: Neurological examination is globally intact with absolutely no focal limb weakness, ataxia, or reflex asymmetry. Pupillary responses are equal, round, and reactive to light. Fundoscopy reveals sharp optic disc margins, completely ruling out papilledema. Cranial nerve testing shows full extraocular muscle movements bilaterally, completely ruling out a cranial nerve VI palsy. An asymmetrical corneal light reflex is objectively demonstrated cleanly during a simple Hirschberg test. A distinct refixation movement of the deviated left eye is seen directly upon completing the alternating cover-uncover test. The diagnostic tetrad of visible ocular misalignment, asymmetrical corneal light reflex, and refixation on the cover test is definitively confirmed.
Environmental Observations: Dimmed room lighting; toys scattered causing a trip hazard for the visually impaired child.
Medication Reconciliation: No medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Strabismus (Accommodative Esotropia) - Status: Acute decompensation of an intermittent condition
Differentials:

Intracranial Mass (Posterior Fossa Tumor) - Ruled out explicitly by the normal fundoscopic exam (no papilledema), normal gait/cerebellar testing, and completely full extraocular movements indicating no true cranial nerve palsy.

Cranial Nerve VI Palsy - Ruled out by the patient's absolute ability to fully abduct the left eye past the midline when the right eye is covered.

Retinoblastoma - Ruled out by the presence of a normal, healthy red reflex bilaterally, lacking the classic leukocoria (white pupillary reflex).
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low (Medical risk: Low, Parental distress: High)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The sudden, acute decompensation of ocular alignment in a toddler necessitated an immediate after-hours neurological assessment. Telephone triage correctly identified the high-stakes need to exclude an acute intracranial space-occupying lesion or acute cranial nerve palsy, which can present precisely as sudden-onset strabismus. Objective clinical evaluation explicitly confirmed full extraocular motility and normal optic discs, completely ruling out a neurosurgical emergency. Delaying this assessment would have resulted in profound, unnecessary parental terror and risked overlooking a genuine neurological catastrophe. Immediate physical evaluation provided absolute diagnostic clarity, safely diagnosing decompensated accommodative esotropia and preventing a highly traumatic emergency department workup.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Reassurance provided regarding the absolute absence of a brain tumor or nerve damage. Generated an urgent referral to a pediatric ophthalmologist.
Positive Behavioral Change: To reduce the child's acute distress and clumsiness, the parents must immediately apply a physical patch over the child's strong (right) eye for brief, 15-minute intervals during highly preferred activities (e.g., watching a cartoon). Rewarding the tolerance of the patch with the high-value activity positively reinforces compliance with the patching, eliminating the double vision and mechanically forcing the brain to utilize the deviated eye's visual pathway, preventing rapid onset amblyopia.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Eye patch.
Follow-up: Urgent pediatric ophthalmology review within 7 days for cycloplegic refraction and formal orthoptic assessment.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transfer to the Emergency Department or call 000 if the child develops a severe headache, begins vomiting forcefully, if the eye becomes completely unable to move outward past the nose, or if the pupil turns white.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Final diagnosis and treatment require formal assessment by a pediatric ophthalmologist.
Clinical Course: The eye crossing is likely due to severe, uncorrected far-sightedness (hyperopia). The child's brain is over-focusing to see clearly, which mechanically drives the eyes to cross inward. Corrective glasses usually straighten the eyes entirely.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Return to the emergency department if the child starts vomiting, complains of severe head pain, or becomes unusually sleepy and difficult to wake.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The sudden eye crossing is a focusing problem, not a brain tumor. The child is likely very far-sighted, and the effort required to focus has caused the eye muscles to spasm inward. The nerves and brain are completely healthy. The clumsiness is simply because the child temporarily has double vision.
Actionable Steps: Keep the floor completely clear of trip hazards tonight. Do not force the child to perform detailed visual tasks like reading or drawing. Ensure prompt attendance at the eye specialist appointment for glasses.

